🌐 Web query:

`bash command substitution inside double quotes $() executes`

💡 Result:

Yes, bash command substitution using the $(command) syntax executes even when placed inside double quotes [1][2]. When the shell encounters a command substitution within double quotes, it performs the following actions: 1. Execution: The command inside the parentheses is executed in a subshell environment [1]. 2. Capture: The standard output of that command is captured, with trailing newlines deleted [1][3]. 3. Substitution: The $(command) construct is replaced by the captured output [1]. 4. Quoting Behavior: Crucially, when the substitution occurs within double quotes, Bash does not perform word splitting or filename expansion (globbing) on the result [1][3]. This preserves whitespace and special characters in the output, which is generally considered the safest and most robust practice in shell scripting [4][5]. In contrast, if command substitution is performed outside of double quotes, the resulting string is subject to word splitting and filename expansion, which can lead to errors if the command's output contains spaces, tabs, newlines, or globbing characters [4][3][5].

Citations:

- 1: https://www.gnu.org/s/bash/manual/html_node/Command-Substitution.html
- 2: https://saturncloud.io/blog/difference-between-single-and-double-quotes-in-bash/
- 3: https://ss64.com/bash/syntax-substitution.html
- 4: https://unix.stackexchange.com/questions/118433/quoting-within-command-substitution-in-bash
- 5: https://dev.to/alanwest/why-your-bash-scripts-keep-breaking-and-how-to-escape-the-trap-4ccp

