🌐 Web query:

`GitHub Actions best practice avoid using ${{ }} directly in run commands use env instead`

💡 Result:

In GitHub Actions, you should avoid using ${{ }} expressions directly within run commands because it exposes your workflow to critical security risks, specifically command injection [1][2][3]. When you use the ${{ }} syntax directly in a run block, GitHub Actions evaluates the expression and performs macro-expansion before the script is executed by the runner [1][3]. If the expression contains untrusted input—such as PR titles, issue comments, or other user-controlled data—an attacker can inject malicious shell commands by including shell metacharacters (e.g.,;, &&, |) [1][2][3]. Because the expression is replaced with literal text before the shell sees it, these injected commands will execute with the same privileges as your workflow [1][3][4]. The recommended best practice is to pass untrusted input through an intermediate environment variable [1][5][2]. How to implement the secure approach: 1. Define an environment variable in the env section of your step. 2. Assign the ${{ }} expression to that environment variable. 3. Reference the environment variable inside your run command using standard shell syntax (e.g., $VARIABLE_NAME) [1][2]. Vulnerable example: - name: Print Title run: echo "Title: ${{ github.event.issue.title }}" Secure example: - name: Print Title env: ISSUE_TITLE: ${{ github.event.issue.title }} run: echo "Title: $ISSUE_TITLE" By using this approach, the input is treated as a literal variable value by the shell, preventing it from being interpreted as executable command logic [1][3]. While this does not eliminate all security risks (the input itself remains untrusted data), it effectively neutralizes the primary vector for command injection by separating data from command execution [1]. Always treat inputs sourced from external contributors with caution and consider additional validation or sanitization when necessary [1][3][4].

Citations:

- 1: https://github.blog/security/supply-chain-security/four-tips-to-keep-your-github-actions-workflows-secure/
- 2: https://github.com/github/gh-aw/issues/9124
- 3: https://github.com/github/docs/blob/main/content/actions/concepts/security/script-injections.md
- 4: https://openssf.org/blog/2024/08/12/mitigating-attack-vectors-in-github-workflows/
- 5: https://github.com/github/docs/issues/41779

